**Question 8 (coding, 12 points)**

Write a function that returns `true` if and only if there are two **different** indices i and j with `|i - j| <= indexDiff` **and** `|nums[i] - nums[j]| <= valueDiff`. Both `indexDiff` and `valueDiff` are >= 0, and the values may be as large as the full range of `int`.

Use an ordered container so that the function runs in O(n log w) time, where w is the number of elements you keep in the window (a sliding window of the previous `indexDiff` elements). Hint: you only need to check **one** candidate from the window per element.

```cpp
bool containsNearbyAlmostDuplicate(const std::vector<int>& nums, int indexDiff, int valueDiff) {
    // write code here
}
```

(9 points for the code.) Then **(3 points)**: give the tight worst-case running time in terms of n = nums.size() and d = indexDiff, with a brief justification.

questions.answer:
```cpp
bool containsNearbyAlmostDuplicate(const std::vector<int>& nums, int indexDiff, int valueDiff) {
    std::set<long long> window;                            // the values of the previous indexDiff positions
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {
        long long x = nums[i];
        auto it = window.lower_bound(x - valueDiff);       // smallest stored value >= x - valueDiff
        if (it != window.end() && *it <= x + valueDiff)    // ...and it is also <= x + valueDiff
            return true;
        window.insert(x);
        if (i >= indexDiff)
            window.erase((long long)nums[i - indexDiff]);  // that position is now too far from i + 1
    }
    return false;
}
```

**Why it works.** The window holds exactly the values at positions i - indexDiff .. i - 1, the only positions within index distance of i. A window value v satisfies |v - x| <= valueDiff iff x - valueDiff <= v <= x + valueDiff. The *smallest* window value that is >= x - valueDiff (found by `lower_bound`) is the best candidate: if it is also <= x + valueDiff we have a match, and if it is not, no window value lies in the interval. Every value is first checked and then inserted, so a duplicate value is caught by the next element (valueDiff >= 0). `long long` avoids overflow in `x - valueDiff` and `x + valueDiff`.

**Complexity.** n iterations; each does one `lower_bound`, one `insert` and at most one `erase` on a balanced BST holding at most min(n, d) + 1 elements: O(log(min(n, d) + 1)) each. Total **O(n log min(n, d))** (O(n log n) when d >= n). Unlike a hash set, the ordered set is required because the test is a range test, not equality. Space O(min(n, d)).

questions.prompt:
**Question 9 (coding, 12 points)**

Write a function that returns the length of the **longest run of consecutive integers** that all appear in `nums` (in any order, duplicates possible). For example, `{100, 4, 200, 1, 3, 2}` gives 4 (the run 1, 2, 3, 4), and an empty vector gives 0.

It must run in **O(n) average time**, so sorting is not allowed. Use `std::unordered_set`. (Assume the values stay clear of INT_MIN and INT_MAX so that x - 1 and x + len do not overflow.)

```cpp
int longestConsecutive(const std::vector<int>& nums) {
    // write code here
}
```

(8 points for the code.) Then **(4 points)**: explain why the running time is O(n) average even though there is a while loop nested inside a for loop, and give the worst case.

questions.answer:
```cpp
int longestConsecutive(const std::vector<int>& nums) {
    std::unordered_set<int> present(nums.begin(), nums.end());   // O(n) average
    int best = 0;
    for (int x : present) {
        if (present.count(x - 1) == 0) {                   // x is the START of a run
            int len = 1;
            while (present.count(x + len) > 0)             // walk the run
                ++len;
            best = std::max(best, len);
        }
    }
    return best;
}
```

**Complexity.** Building the set from the n numbers: O(n) average. The outer loop visits each distinct value once and does one O(1) average `count(x - 1)`. The inner `while` only runs for a value `x` that **starts a run** (x - 1 is absent), and it walks exactly that run. Each value belongs to exactly one run, so across the whole outer loop every value is walked over by the inner loop **at most once**: the inner loop does at most d <= n steps in total (not n steps per outer iteration). Total O(n) + O(n) + O(n) = **O(n) average**. In the worst case every hash operation degrades to O(n) (all values collide) and the function is **O(n^2)**. Space O(n).

Trap: starting the walk from *every* x (without the `x - 1` test) makes the inner loop re-walk the same suffix of a run again and again: O(n^2) even with a perfect hash.
